Electrum is a Qt app, so there isn’t really a “Qt vs Electrum UI” choice — you’ll use Qt widgets, but it’s smart to lean on Electrum’s helper classes so your dialog looks and feels native.

Let me break it down by:

How to embed this in Electrum

Which specific widgets to use for each part of the visualization

Where to use Electrum helpers vs raw Qt

1. Where this lives in Electrum

You basically have two good options:

Option A – Separate dialog (easiest to start with)

Triggered from a menu item / right-click → “View spending conditions…”

Implemented as a WindowModalDialog (Electrum wrapper around QDialog):

from electrum.gui.qt.util import WindowModalDialog

class MiniscriptDialog(WindowModalDialog):
    def __init__(self, parent, wallet, script_info):
        super().__init__(parent, _("Spending conditions"))
        # build layouts and widgets here


Why this is nice:

No need to touch Electrum’s main tab layout.

You can open it for a specific UTXO, address, or script.

Option B – New tab in the main window (more integrated)

Add a “Policy” or “Scripts” tab to the main ElectrumWindow (QTabWidget).

Use a normal QWidget with layouts.

If you’re just prototyping, start with Option A. You can always migrate the core widget into a tab later.

2. Recommended widgets for your visualization

Think of your UI in 3 sections: script info, tree view, simulator controls.

2.1 Layout containers

Use Qt layouts:

QVBoxLayout for vertical stacking.

QHBoxLayout for side-by-side sections.

QSplitter if you want resizable panes (tree left, simulator right).

Rough structure in the dialog:

main_layout = QVBoxLayout(self)

# Top: script info
top_widget = QWidget()
top_layout = QVBoxLayout(top_widget)
main_layout.addWidget(top_widget)

# Bottom: split tree + simulator
splitter = QSplitter()
main_layout.addWidget(splitter)

left = QWidget()
left_layout = QVBoxLayout(left)
splitter.addWidget(left)

right = QWidget()
right_layout = QVBoxLayout(right)
splitter.addWidget(right)

2.2 Script info (top area)

Use a mix of labels and a read-only text edit.

Widgets:

QLabel for “Descriptor:”, “Address:”.

ButtonsTextEdit (Electrum helper) or QPlainTextEdit set to read-only for the descriptor/Miniscript.

Example:

from electrum.gui.qt.util import ButtonsTextEdit

descriptor_edit = ButtonsTextEdit()
descriptor_edit.setReadOnly(True)
descriptor_edit.setText(descriptor_str)
top_layout.addWidget(QLabel(_("Descriptor")))
top_layout.addWidget(descriptor_edit)

2.3 Tree / “control block” visualization (bottom left)

Simplest and most robust: tree view first, fancy graphics later.

Use QTreeWidget (or QTreeView + model)

Each AST node becomes a QTreeWidgetItem.

The hierarchy naturally expresses AND/OR/THRESH structure.

You can color items based on satisfaction state.

tree = QTreeWidget()
tree.setHeaderHidden(True)  # we don’t really need columns
left_layout.addWidget(tree)


Populate it from your AST:

and node → parent item “AND”

or node → parent item “OR”

thresh → “k-of-n” label

leaves → “Signature: Alice”, “After block 800000”, etc.

Optional: use icons.

from electrum.gui.qt.util import read_QIcon

key_icon = read_QIcon("key.png")
clock_icon = read_QIcon("clock.png")
hash_icon = read_QIcon("hash.png")


Set them per item:

item.setIcon(0, key_icon)
item.setToolTip(0, miniscript_fragment)


Later, if you want Elephant-style block graphics, you can:

Use QGraphicsView + QGraphicsScene for colored rectangles and arrows.

But I’d get the QTreeWidget version working first — much less pain.

2.4 Simulator panel (bottom right)

Here you want simple form-style controls:

Use QFormLayout + standard widgets

QSpinBox or QLineEdit for current block height.

Another QSpinBox for confirmation height (for relative timelocks).

QGroupBox + QVBoxLayout + QCheckBox for signatures.

QLineEdit for hash preimages.

A big status QLabel at the bottom.

Example skeleton:

from PyQt5.QtWidgets import (
    QFormLayout, QSpinBox, QGroupBox, QVBoxLayout, QCheckBox, QLabel, QLineEdit
)

form = QFormLayout()
right_layout.addLayout(form)

# Block heights
current_height_spin = QSpinBox()
current_height_spin.setMaximum(5000000)
form.addRow(_("Current block height:"), current_height_spin)

confirm_height_spin = QSpinBox()
confirm_height_spin.setMaximum(5000000)
form.addRow(_("Confirmed at height:"), confirm_height_spin)

# Signatures group
sigs_group = QGroupBox(_("Available signatures"))
sigs_layout = QVBoxLayout(sigs_group)
for label in signer_labels:  # e.g. ["Alice", "Bob"]
    cb = QCheckBox(label)
    sigs_layout.addWidget(cb)
right_layout.addWidget(sigs_group)

# Hash preimages
hash_group = QGroupBox(_("Hash preimages"))
hash_layout = QFormLayout(hash_group)
for h in hashes:  # list of known hash locks
    edit = QLineEdit()
    hash_layout.addRow(h.short_label, edit)
right_layout.addWidget(hash_group)

# Result label
result_label = QLabel(_("Result: (not evaluated)"))
right_layout.addWidget(result_label)


Then connect signals:

current_height_spin.valueChanged.connect(self.recompute_satisfaction)
confirm_height_spin.valueChanged.connect(self.recompute_satisfaction)
cb.stateChanged.connect(self.recompute_satisfaction)
edit.textChanged.connect(self.recompute_satisfaction)


And in recompute_satisfaction() you:

Evaluate your AST with this state.

Color tree items (green/red/grey).

Update result_label.

3. Electrum helpers you should reuse

Electrum has some convenience stuff in electrum.gui.qt.util that you should use so you get consistent styling and behavior:

WindowModalDialog – base class for modal dialogs.

Buttons, OkButton, CloseButton, CancelButton – for the bottom button row.

ButtonsTextEdit – text edit with copy/save buttons.

read_QIcon, ColorScheme – consistent icons and colors.

_() – translation wrapper.

Example for dialog buttons:

from electrum.gui.qt.util import Buttons, CloseButton

buttons = Buttons(CloseButton(self))
main_layout.addLayout(buttons)


You don’t need a special Electrum widget for the tree/simulator; standard Qt is perfectly fine there.

4. So: “Electrum UI” or “Qt directly”?

In practice:

Use Qt directly for the core widgets/layout:

QTreeWidget, QSpinBox, QCheckBox, QLineEdit, QFormLayout, etc.

Use Electrum’s wrappers for integration & polish:

WindowModalDialog, ButtonsTextEdit, Buttons, icons, color scheme.

That gives you:

Minimal friction (normal PyQt code).

A UI that looks like it belongs in Electrum.

A clear path to later move the central widget into a tab if you want.

